            <div xml:id="d468">

                <pb facs="0446" n="381" sameAs="frus:frus1936v03/pg381"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">891.711/13</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Postmaster General</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Howes</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-07-02">July 2, 1936</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: I have your letter, NE
                        891.711/3[<hi rendition="#italic">9</hi>], of June 22, 1936, relative to the
                    action of the Iranian Postal Authorities in placing a prohibition on the
                    delivery of second-class publications from the United States due to the
                    appearance in some of said publications of matter deemed by the Iranian
                    Government to be of an objectionable nature.</p>
                <p>While this Department would have no objection to representations on the subject
                    being made to the Government of Iran through diplomatic channels, if you deem
                    proper, it is pointed out that under the provisions of the Universal Postal
                    Convention which governs the exchange of international mails, the Postal
                    Administration of Iran has a right to consider the publications in question as
                    prohibited articles and to treat the same in accordance with its domestic
                    regulations.</p>
                <p>Very truly yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">W. W. Howes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
